reviewer_comments.reviewer_1:
In the present manuscript, the authors theoretically propose and experimentally demonstrate a nanophotonic platform (an anisotropic and nonlocal metasurface) capable to perform reconfigurable circularly polarized thermal emission in the mid-infrared regime. The proposed system enables dynamic and fine control over several key features of thermal emission, including temporal (wavelength-tunable), spatial (highly directional), and circular polarization (helicity-switchable) coherence properties. The main results of the paper can be summarized as follows:
- Fundamental aspect: realization and demonstration of an insightful mechanism, based on the thermo-optic effect, enabling the emission and switching of circularly polarized thermal radiation with dynamic control of coherence properties, without requiring structural reconfiguration;
- Experimental aspect: experimental validation of the proposed concept, showing excellent agreement between numerical simulations and practical measurements over a broad wavelength window and temperature range;
- Applied aspect: compact and simple structural design, together with robustness against fabrication imperfections, thereby suggesting feasible and scalable realistic implementation of the proposed system in a single device.
In my opinion, these results represent an original and significant contribution to the fields of nanophotonics, thermal emission, and metamaterials, both from fundamental and applied perspectives, paving the way toward new avenues for controlling thermal emission properties and developing spin-dependent thermal functionalities. Overall, the manuscript is very well written, and despite its technical depth, the main ideas are clearly conveyed and easy to follow. I found it a pleasure to read and therefore strongly recommend this work for publication in Nature Communications in its current form.
Nevertheless, to further improve the manuscript, I suggest the authors address the following minor points:
1. For completeness, the reference list in the Introduction section could be slightly expanded and updated by considering some recent and relevant works in the area of thermal photonics and circularly polarized thermal emission, for example:
- C. Khandekar and Z. Jacob, Phys. Rev. Appl. 12, 014053 (2019);
- T. Liu et al., eLight 2, 25 (2022);
- X. Wang and Z. Jacob, Light Sci. Appl. 11, 342 (2022);
- X. Wang et al., Sci. Adv. 9, eade4203 (2023);
- M. F. Picardi et al., J. Appl. Phys. 133, 111101 (2023).
Of course, authors are free to considering whether including (or not) some of the above-mentioned references to improve the quality and clarity of their explanations.
2. The choice of material appears to play a crucial role in enabling the thermal tuning mechanism. Since the analysis only focuses on Ge due to its large thermo-optic coefficient, a brief discussion of alternative material platforms, including their potential advantages and limitations, would be valuable.
3. The authors state that the results can be extrapolated to higher temperatures. Conversely, it would be interesting to comment on the behavior at lower temperatures, particularly approaching the zero-temperature limit, where the thermo-optic effect may become negligible. A brief discussion on this issue could provide additional physical insight.
4. Finally, a few minor typographical errors should be corrected:
- Line 129: "[...] cyclic arrows in Fig. 1a [...]", should read as "[...] cyclic arrows in Fig. 1c [...]";
- Line 149: "[...] refractive index based on based on spectral [...]", should read as "[...] refractive index based on spectral [...]";
- Line 283: "[...] regions in Figs. 5b and c highlight [...]", should read as "[...] regions in Figs. 5e and h highlight [...]".
Respectfully, I hope these comments may help the authors further improve the manuscript.
